<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Panaretos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1320–after 1390</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Panaretos was a Greek official and historian of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in the fourteenth century, who served Emperor Alexios III at court and on embassies. His short chronicle, covering the empire from its foundation in 1204 to about 1390, is the only surviving native history of Trebizond and the main source for its court and civil wars.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>All that is known of Panaretos comes from his chronicle. He held the titles of protosebastos and protonotarios under Alexios III Komnenos. He first appears in 1351, accompanying the emperor's mother, Irene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, to Limnia against the rebel Constantine Doranites, and after the civil war he joined Alexios III in a disastrous attack on Cheriana, from which he barely escaped alive. In April 1363 he names himself as a member of an embassy with the megas logothetes George Scholaris to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, to arrange a marriage between a daughter of Alexios and a son of John V Palaiologos; they also met John VI Kantakuzenos.</p><p>The only personal events he records are the deaths of two sons in 1368, while he was away in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>: Constantine drowned at fifteen and Romanos died of disease at seventeen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The chronicle fills only about twenty printed pages. About half covers the years 1349 to 1390, within his adult life. He names no sources; Jakob Philipp Fallmerayer suggested that he copied the inscriptions of a frescoed hall of imperial portraits described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarion">Bessarion</a>, and reign dates that do not always agree with reign lengths point to at least two written sources. Panaretos wrote not in learned Attic Greek but in the Pontic Greek of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, and called his people Romaioi or Christians, never <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Later hands added entries up to 1426, and Anthony Bryer thought a gap near the end once told of the murder of Alexios IV Megas Komnenos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The text survives in one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, Marcianus graecus 608, dated by Peter Schreiner to the 1440s, which reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biblioteca-marciana">Biblioteca Marciana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> through the bequest of Giambattisti Recanti. Fallmerayer found it there in the 19th century. Gottlieb Tafel printed the first edition in 1832, Fallmerayer added a German translation in 1844, and critical editions followed by the Greek scholar Lambros in 1907 and Odysseus Lampsides in 1958; Scott Kennedy published an English translation in 2019. Alexander Vasiliev wrote that this drab but truthful chronicle made it possible to restore the order of events in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>'s history, and it also holds valuable material on the early history of the Ottoman Turks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael Panaretos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A chronicle by <b>Michael Panaretos</b>, an official of Alexios III, preserves the history of the fourteenth-century court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
